# At the Tamil Nadu Music Association Festival
Tamil title: தமிழ்நாட்டுச் சங்கீத சங்க விழாவில்

Speech delivered at a music festival in 1967:

There is a fundamental difference between the artistic distinction of other countries and that of our country. In other countries, art is fostered amid material prosperity. In our country, however, art is fostered even amid poverty! If the people of our country, even in conditions of poverty, savour art, felicitate and nurture artists, and enable art to flourish, then imagining how they would honour and praise the artists who foster art if the people of this country enjoyed material abundance creates the conviction that “a good future lies ahead.” This is the fundamental difference between the fostering of art in other countries and in ours.

Artists working in every branch of art not only develop their respective fields with refinement but also perform fine work in a manner worthy of praise by people of other countries. Those in music, dance, and other fields have spent a major part of their lives in poverty. I take pride in the fact that those who give the country its wealth of art have received awards.

Cinema is the field in which all these ancient arts have been brought together in a modern form. When fashioned with refinement, cinema is an outstanding instrument capable of producing tenderness and joy. Distinguished artists in such a field have received awards, and I convey my congratulations and praise to them.

An award has been given in appreciation of Brother Karunanidhi’s success in the field of screen story and dialogue. There may be people who entertain the futile doubt whether his selection as a minister was right or wrong, but no one can have even the slightest doubt about the conferral of this award! Such is the distinction of his involvement in this field.

The world of art is also delighted to know that, while safeguarding artistic refinement, he is carrying out the responsibility entrusted to him with that same refinement. Some who do one thing while thinking of another imagine themselves to be artists. But just as Kalaignar Karunanidhi engages in art and performs it with refinement, he is also combining artistic refinement with governance, carrying out his work, and making progress. I trust that you will permit me to take personal pride in such a person receiving this award.

Editorial request: If readers can obtain the speech delivered by Arignar Anna at the first Tamil Isai conference in 1943, they are requested to send it to Poompuhar.

---NOTES---
The year is OCRed as “1267”; the ministerial context and printed introduction support 1967, used in English and logged separately.
The final request is editorial material printed with the speech, not part of Anna’s address; it is labelled accordingly rather than omitted.
